# Truffle — local test & benchmark environment.
# Copy to `.env` (which is gitignored) and fill in values.
#
# Without TRUFFLE_TEST_AUTHKEY set, real-network tests and benchmarks
# will skip gracefully. Mock-based tests and micro benchmarks always run.
#
# How to get an auth key:
#   1. Go to https://login.tailscale.com/admin/settings/keys
#   2. Generate an auth key with: Reusable, Ephemeral, Tags: tag:truffle-test
#      (and Pre-approved if device approval is enabled on your tailnet)
#   3. Ensure your tailnet ACL grants `tag:truffle-test` as an ownerless tag.
#      See docs/TESTING.md for a snippet.
#
# See docs/TESTING.md for the full walkthrough.

# REQUIRED for real-network tests & benches. Leave unset to skip them.
TRUFFLE_TEST_AUTHKEY=

# Optional overrides (defaults shown):
# TRUFFLE_TEST_TAGS=      # comma-separated tags to advertise. LEAVE UNSET
                          # unless your tailnet ACL has a matching tagOwners
                          # entry — the auth key's own tag applies
                          # automatically. Set only for explicit overrides.
# TRUFFLE_TEST_EPHEMERAL=true
# TRUFFLE_TEST_LOG=truffle_core=info
# TRUFFLE_TEST_STATE_ROOT=

# Benchmark hub (RFC 027, benchmark/README.md). Optional.
# truffle-bench looks for a key in this order: --auth-key-file (first line),
# TRUFFLE_BENCH_AUTH_KEY, TRUFFLE_TEST_AUTHKEY, TS_AUTHKEY (warns). Keys are
# never accepted on the command line.
#   - Exception: the legacy `truffle-bench transport` subcommand reads only
#     TRUFFLE_TEST_AUTHKEY, then TS_AUTHKEY, and has no --auth-key-file. Keep
#     TRUFFLE_TEST_AUTHKEY (above) set if you use it.
#   - `truffle-bench serve`, `peers` and `run` work without a key: they print a
#     login URL and you sign in with your own Tailscale account.
#   - `truffle-bench pair` and `suite --pair` need a key (the same test key works).
#   - The truffle bench app reads TRUFFLE_BENCH_AUTH_KEY from its environment
#     to sign in without a browser (dev and test only).
# A tagged key (like the test key) makes the device a tagged device. A responder
# admits a tagged initiator only if it is tagged too and allows tagged peers
# (--allow-tagged, the default when it has a key), or with --allow-other-logins.
# TRUFFLE_BENCH_AUTH_KEY=
